<h1>Conveyor Companies Near You</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Finding the right conveyor company depends on what you need: a single conveyor purchase, a complete system design, or ongoing service and maintenance. Local and regional companies offer faster response times and lower travel costs, while national manufacturers and integrators bring broader product lines and deeper engineering resources. This guide covers where to search for conveyor companies by region, how to evaluate them based on capability and experience, and what questions to ask before committing to a project. For a directory of manufacturers organized by conveyor type, see our <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">manufacturer directory</a>.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/curve-interroll-belt-curve.webp" width="1000" height="563" alt="Product image of a 90-degree belt curve with a black belt, black side frames, low side guards, and a gearmotor mounted at one end." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A 90-degree belt curve module. The belt forms a fan shape, wider at the outside radius, and the drive sits at one end of the curve. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.interroll.com/products/belt-curves" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Interroll</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="search-heading">Where to Search</h2>
<h3>Industry Directories</h3>
<p>The most efficient starting point is industry-specific directories. The <a href="https://www.cemanet.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Conveyor Equipment Manufacturers Association (CEMA)</a> publishes an online <a href="https://members.cemanet.org/member-directory" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">member directory</a>. <a href="https://www.mhi.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MHI</a> covers a broader scope, including conveyors, AGVs, robotics, and warehouse automation. Thomasnet provides company profiles with certifications and capabilities. The <a href="https://www.pmmi.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PMMI</a> membership covers packaging machinery manufacturers, many of whom build or integrate conveyors.</p>
<h3>Manufacturer Dealer Locators</h3>
<p>If you already know the conveyor brand you want, check the manufacturer's website for a dealer or integration partner locator. Hytrol, for example, sells through a network of integration partners and offers an online partner locator. Dorner lists distributors on its site, and mk North America offers a sales rep locator. Authorized partners get direct manufacturer support, and many stock common spare parts. Ask any partner which parts they keep on the shelf.</p>
<h3>Trade Show Exhibitor Lists</h3>
<p><a href="https://www.packexpo.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PACK EXPO</a> International (Chicago, even years) and PACK EXPO Las Vegas (odd years), <a href="https://www.promatshow.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ProMat</a> (Chicago, odd years), and <a href="https://www.modexshow.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MODEX</a> (Atlanta, even years) are major North American trade shows for conveyor equipment. Exhibitor lists are published online before each show. Even if you do not attend, the exhibitor directory is a list of active conveyor companies, usually searchable by product category, often with company descriptions and booth sizes that hint at relative market presence.</p>
<h3>Targeted Web Search</h3>
<p>For local companies that may not appear in national directories, search for specific terms: "conveyor integrator [city/state]", "packaging conveyor [region]", "belt conveyor manufacturer [state]", or "material handling company [metro area]". Local industrial contractors, machine builders, and automation companies sometimes build or integrate conveyors as part of a broader service offering, they may not appear in conveyor-specific directories but can be strong regional partners.</p>
<h3>Referrals From Adjacent Suppliers</h3>
<p>Your existing equipment suppliers, packaging machine OEMs, robot integrators, controls panel shops, and industrial electricians, often know the best conveyor companies in your region. They work alongside conveyor builders on projects and can recommend companies based on firsthand experience with their quality, reliability, and service. Similarly, your industry peers at competitor or non-competitor facilities in the same region can share their conveyor supplier experiences.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Types of Conveyor Companies</h2>
<p>The term "conveyor company" covers several distinct business models. Understanding the differences helps you contact the right type of company for your needs.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Company Type</th>
<th>What They Do</th>
<th>Best For</th>
<th>Limitations</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Manufacturer (OEM)</td>
<td>Design and build conveyor equipment in their own facility</td>
<td>Single conveyor purchases, standard products, <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/custom/">custom builds</a></td>
<td>May not handle installation, controls integration, or multi-vendor systems</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>System Integrator</td>
<td>Design complete systems, select equipment, manage controls, install, and commission</td>
<td>Multi-conveyor systems, complex automation, turnkey projects</td>
<td>Higher project cost due to engineering and PM overhead; may favor certain brands</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Dealer / Rep</td>
<td>Represent one or a few brands, provide sales and sometimes installation</td>
<td>Buying a specific brand, local sales support, warranty coordination</td>
<td>Limited to the brands they represent; may not do controls or custom engineering</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Distributor</td>
<td>Stock and sell conveyor components, belting, rollers, motors, drives, <a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">parts</a></td>
<td>Replacement parts, belting, components for in-house builds</td>
<td>Do not design or build conveyor systems; sell components only</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Service Company</td>
<td>Maintain, repair, and rebuild existing conveyor systems</td>
<td>Preventive maintenance contracts, emergency repairs, belt replacement</td>
<td>Do not design or build new systems; focus on maintenance and repair</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2 id="eval-heading">How to Evaluate a Conveyor Company</h2>
<h3>Industry Experience</h3>
<p>A company that has completed multiple projects in your industry understands the application-specific challenges that a general-purpose builder may overlook. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food industry</a> conveyors require sanitary design knowledge. <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">Pharmaceutical</a> applications require cleanroom protocols. <a href="/industries/automotive-conveyors/">Automotive</a> plants need heavy-duty construction and precise timing. Ask for a project list or case studies in your industry, not just a general portfolio.</p>
<h3>References You Can Contact</h3>
<p>Testimonials on a website are marketing. References you can call are data. Ask for several references from recent projects similar to yours: same industry and similar scope. When you call references, ask about: Did the project meet the original timeline and budget? How was communication during the project? Were there issues, and how were they resolved? How is the equipment performing now? Would you hire them again? The willingness to provide references is itself informative, a company that hesitates may have reasons.</p>
<h3>In-House Capabilities</h3>
<p>Determine what the company does in-house versus what they subcontract. Key capabilities to assess: mechanical design and CAD, fabrication (welding, machining, assembly), controls engineering (PLC programming, HMI development, VFD/servo setup), electrical panel building, installation crews, and commissioning. Companies that subcontract heavily may deliver good results, but coordination complexity increases and accountability can become diffuse when problems arise.</p>
<h3>Service and Support</h3>
<p>Evaluate post-installation support: What is the warranty period and coverage? Do they offer preventive maintenance contracts? What is their typical response time for emergency service calls? Do they stock spare parts locally? Can they provide remote diagnostics (phone, video, remote PLC access) for initial troubleshooting before dispatching a technician? A company that builds conveyors but does not support them after installation leaves you dependent on third-party service providers or your own maintenance team.</p>
<h3>Financial Stability</h3>
<p>Conveyor projects can span months from order to commissioning, and the warranty period runs after that. A company's financial stability affects their ability to complete your project, honor their warranty, and provide parts and service for years to come. For larger projects, request a D&amp;B report or check for any recent ownership changes, acquisitions, or restructuring. Companies with a long operating history and stable ownership tend to provide more consistent long-term support.</p>
<h2 id="regional-heading">Regional Conveyor Landscape</h2>
<p>Conveyor manufacturing and integration companies cluster in regions with strong manufacturing bases. The concentration of companies in your region affects availability, competition, and pricing.</p>
<h3>Midwest (OH, MI, IN, WI, IL, MN)</h3>
<p>A dense cluster of conveyor manufacturers and integrators. Strong in food processing, <a href="/industries/assembly-line-conveyors/">assembly line</a>, automotive, and general manufacturing conveyors. Companies include Dorner (WI), Nercon (WI), Toyota Automated Logistics (formerly Bastian Solutions, IN), and many regional integrators. Buyers here usually have multiple options for most conveyor types.</p>
<h3>Southeast (GA, TN, NC, SC, AL, AR)</h3>
<p>Growing manufacturing base with strong logistics and distribution sector. Hytrol (AR) is headquartered here, and their integration partner network covers the region extensively. Dematic is headquartered in Atlanta, which also hosts MODEX and many <a href="/industries/warehouse-conveyors/">warehouse</a> and distribution conveyor companies. Automotive manufacturing growth in the Southeast has brought conveyor companies to support new plants.</p>
<h3>Northeast (PA, NJ, NY, CT, MA)</h3>
<p>Dense manufacturing corridor with established conveyor companies. mk North America and Conveyco (both CT) are headquartered here. Strong in pharmaceutical, food, and consumer goods conveyors. Higher labor costs are reflected in project pricing, but proximity to major markets and skilled labor pool are advantages. Several companies specialize in pharmaceutical and <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/cleanroom/">cleanroom conveyors</a> for the region's biotech and pharma clusters.</p>
<h3>West Coast (CA, OR, WA)</h3>
<p>Fewer conveyor manufacturers compared to the Midwest, but a strong base of integrators serving food processing (Central Valley), tech/e-commerce distribution (Pacific NW), and aerospace. Heat and Control (Hayward, CA) serves the food processing sector. Higher cost of business is reflected in pricing. Some companies import from Midwest manufacturers and provide local installation and service.</p>
<h3>Texas &amp; Southwest</h3>
<p>Rapidly growing manufacturing and distribution market. Dallas-Fort Worth and Houston metro areas have multiple conveyor companies serving food, beverage, distribution, and oil/gas industries. Laughlin Conveyor (Fort Worth) builds sanitary stainless conveyors. Lower cost of living and business-friendly environment have attracted both manufacturers and integrators. Strong in heavy-duty and outdoor conveyor applications for energy and mining sectors.</p>
<h3>Canada</h3>
<p>Ontario and Quebec have the highest concentration of conveyor companies, serving automotive (Ontario), food processing, and mining. Western Canada has conveyor companies focused on mining, oil sands, and agriculture. Cross-border purchasing from US manufacturers is common, though duty, freight, and CSA/UL certification requirements add cost and complexity.</p>
<h2 id="local-heading">Local vs. National Companies</h2>
<h3>Choose Local When...</h3>
<ul>
<li>•The project requires frequent on-site visits during design and installation</li>
<li>•You need fast emergency service response</li>
<li>•The project involves integration with existing equipment that requires site surveys</li>
<li>•You want to visit the fabrication shop during manufacturing</li>
<li>•You need ongoing preventive maintenance support</li>
<li>•The local company has specific expertise in your industry and application</li>
</ul>
<h3>Choose National When...</h3>
<ul>
<li>•You need a specific conveyor type or technology that local companies do not offer</li>
<li>•You are buying a standard catalog conveyor with minimal customization</li>
<li>•You need multiple conveyor systems across facilities in different regions</li>
<li>•The project requires engineering depth (FEA, simulation, advanced controls) that local companies cannot provide</li>
<li>•Your maintenance team can handle routine service without vendor support</li>
<li>•No qualified local company exists for your conveyor type or industry</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="rfq-heading">The RFQ Process</h2>
<h3>Prepare a Consistent Specification</h3>
<p>Send the same specification document to every vendor. This is the single most important step in getting comparable quotes. Without a consistent spec, each vendor will quote a different scope, making comparison impossible. Include product data, throughput requirements, environment conditions, controls scope, installation scope, and timeline requirements. Our <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/custom/">custom design guide</a> includes a detailed specification checklist.</p>
<h3>Request Two or Three Quotes Minimum</h3>
<p>Two quotes tell you there is a range. Three quotes show you where the market center is and which vendor is an outlier. Beyond a handful, extra quotes add evaluation work with little new information. Choose vendors that represent different approaches, one manufacturer, one integrator, one local company, to see how different business models price the same scope.</p>
<h3>Compare Scope, Not Just Price</h3>
<p>The lowest price is not always the best value. When comparing quotes, check what is included and excluded: Is installation included, and what does "installation" mean (mechanical only, or electrical and commissioning too)? Are controls included, or quoted separately? What is the warranty coverage and duration? Are startup and operator training included? Are <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">infeed/outfeed</a> transitions included, or are they your responsibility? A detailed scope comparison often reveals that the apparent low-price quote is missing significant scope items.</p>
<h3>Negotiate on Value, Not Just Price</h3>
<p>Beyond the purchase price, negotiable items include: extended warranty, spare parts included in the order, preventive maintenance contract pricing, payment terms (milestone-based vs. upfront), documentation deliverables, performance guarantees with penalties, and future expansion pricing. A vendor who provides a comprehensive spare parts package and a two-year warranty at a slightly higher price may deliver better total value than the lowest bidder with a one-year warranty and no spares. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">cost guide</a> for factors that drive conveyor pricing.</p>
